export default "# Networking for Cybersecurity: TCP/IP Model and Network Communication\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: FOUNDATIONS OF THE TCP/IP MODEL\n\n## 1. Introduction to Network Communication\n\nEvery time you open a website, send a WhatsApp message, connect to a server, or download a file, your device communicates with another device through a network.\n\nThis communication follows established rules called **network protocols**.\n\nA network protocol defines how devices exchange information.\n\nFor example:\n\n- HTTP defines how web clients and servers exchange web requests and responses.\n- TCP provides reliable, ordered delivery of data.\n- IP handles addressing and routing between networks.\n- DNS translates domain names into IP addresses.\n- Ethernet defines communication over many wired local networks.\n- ARP helps discover the hardware address associated with an IPv4 address on a local network.\n\nUnderstanding these protocols is essential in cybersecurity because attackers and defenders both interact with network communication.\n\nA cybersecurity professional needs to understand:\n\n- How information moves between devices.\n- How IP addresses identify network destinations.\n- How ports identify services.\n- How protocols establish communication.\n- How attackers intercept or manipulate network traffic.\n- How defenders detect suspicious connections.\n- How firewalls control network communication.\n\n### Real-world example\n\nSuppose you open:\n\n```text\nhttps://example.com\n```\n\nYour computer performs several operations:\n\n1. Determines the IP address associated with `example.com`.\n2. Establishes network communication with the destination.\n3. Establishes a secure connection using TLS.\n4. Sends an HTTP request.\n5. Receives the server's response.\n6. Displays the webpage.\n\nThese operations involve multiple networking protocols working together.\n\n## 2. What Is the TCP/IP Model?\n\nThe **TCP/IP model** is a networking framework that describes how data is communicated between devices across interconnected networks.\n\nTCP/IP stands for:\n\n- Transmission Control Protocol.\n- Internet Protocol.\n\nThe model groups networking operations into four layers.\n\nEach layer performs a particular responsibility and works with the layers above and below it.\n\n### The Four TCP/IP Layers\n\n| Layer | Main responsibility | Example protocols or technologies |\n|---|---|---|\n| Application | Provides network services to applications | HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, SMTP, SSH |\n| Transport | Provides communication between processes | TCP, UDP |\n| Internet | Provides logical addressing and routing | IPv4, IPv6, ICMP |\n| Network Access | Handles local network delivery and physical transmission | Ethernet, Wi-Fi, ARP |\n\nThe model is important because network communication is not one single operation.\n\nIt is a combination of different operations working together.\n\n### Understanding the layers through an example\n\nImagine accessing a website.\n\n**Application layer**\n\nYour browser creates an HTTP request.\n\n**Transport layer**\n\nTCP establishes a connection and manages reliable data delivery when TCP is used.\n\n**Internet layer**\n\nIP adds source and destination IP addresses so routers can forward the packet.\n\n**Network Access layer**\n\nThe data is transmitted over the local network using an appropriate link technology, such as Ethernet or Wi-Fi.\n\nAt the receiving end, the process is reversed.\n\nThis process is called **decapsulation**.\n\n## 3. Understanding Encapsulation\n\nEncapsulation is the process of adding protocol information to data as it moves down the networking layers.\n\nEach layer may add a header containing information needed for communication.\n\nConsider an HTTPS request sent from a laptop to a web server.\n\nThe simplified process is:\n\n```text\nApplication Data\n       |\n       v\nTransport Header + Data\n       |\n       v\nIP Header + Segment\n       |\n       v\nNetwork Frame\n       |\n       v\nPhysical Transmission\n```\n\nThe actual terminology depends on the protocol and layer.\n\n| Layer | Common data unit |\n|---|---|\n| Application | Data or message |\n| Transport | TCP segment or UDP datagram |\n| Internet | IP packet |\n| Network Access | Frame |\n| Physical medium | Bits or signals |\n\n### Example: Encapsulation in practice\n\nSuppose your browser sends:\n\n```http\nGET /products HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\n```\n\nThe application creates the HTTP message.\n\nIf TCP is used:\n\n1. TCP adds source and destination port information.\n2. TCP may add sequence and acknowledgment information.\n3. IP adds source and destination IP addresses.\n4. The local network protocol adds link-layer addressing and frame information.\n5. The data is transmitted through the network.\n\nAt the destination, the receiving system processes the headers and passes the data to the appropriate protocol or application.\n\n### Why encapsulation matters in cybersecurity\n\nEncapsulation helps security professionals understand where an attack or communication problem occurs.\n\nFor example:\n\n- A malicious HTTP request may be an application-layer issue.\n- A TCP SYN flood targets transport-layer connection handling.\n- IP spoofing manipulates source IP information.\n- ARP spoofing manipulates local network address resolution.\n\nDifferent attacks target different parts of network communication.\n\n## 4. Application Layer\n\nThe application layer is the top layer of the TCP/IP model.\n\nIt provides network communication services to applications.\n\nExamples include browsers, email clients, remote administration tools and file transfer applications.\n\n### Important application-layer protocols\n\n| Protocol | Purpose | Common port |\n|---|---|---:|\n| HTTP | Transfers web content | TCP 80 |\n| HTTPS | Secure web communication | TCP 443 |\n| DNS | Resolves domain names | UDP/TCP 53 |\n| SSH | Secure remote administration | TCP 22 |\n| FTP | File transfer | TCP 21 |\n| SMTP | Sends email | TCP 25 |\n| POP3 | Retrieves email | TCP 110 |\n| IMAP | Accesses and synchronizes email | TCP 143 |\n| DHCP | Assigns network configuration | UDP 67/68 |\n\nPorts listed here are common defaults. Services can be configured to use different ports.\n\n### Example: HTTP communication\n\nWhen you visit a website, your browser may send:\n\n```http\nGET /index.html HTTP/1.1\nHost: example.com\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```http\nGET\n```\n\nSpecifies the HTTP method used to request a resource.\n\n```http\n/index.html\n```\n\nIdentifies the requested resource path.\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1\n```\n\nIdentifies the HTTP protocol version.\n\n```http\nHost: example.com\n```\n\nIdentifies the requested hostname.\n\nThe server processes the request and returns an HTTP response.\n\nExample:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html\n```\n\n`200 OK` indicates that the request was successfully processed.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nApplication-layer security issues include:\n\n- SQL injection.\n- Cross-site scripting (XSS).\n- Insecure authentication.\n- Broken access control.\n- Directory traversal.\n- Insecure API endpoints.\n- Malicious file uploads.\n\nSecurity professionals examine application requests and responses to identify these vulnerabilities.\n\n## 5. Transport Layer\n\nThe transport layer provides communication between applications or processes running on different devices.\n\nTwo major transport protocols are:\n\n- TCP.\n- UDP.\n\n### Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)\n\nTCP is a connection-oriented transport protocol.\n\nIt provides mechanisms for:\n\n- Reliable data delivery.\n- Ordered data delivery.\n- Error detection.\n- Retransmission of lost data.\n- Flow control.\n- Congestion control.\n\nTCP is commonly used by web applications, SSH and many database connections.\n\n### Understanding TCP communication\n\nBefore exchanging normal application data, TCP generally establishes a connection using a process called the **three-way handshake**.\n\nThe three stages are:\n\n1. SYN.\n2. SYN-ACK.\n3. ACK.\n\nDiagram:\n\n```text\nClient                         Server\n  |                               |\n  |---------- SYN --------------->|\n  |                               |\n  |<--------- SYN-ACK ------------|\n  |                               |\n  |---------- ACK --------------->|\n  |                               |\n  |       Connection Ready        |\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n**Step 1: SYN**\n\nThe client sends a SYN packet to request a TCP connection.\n\n**Step 2: SYN-ACK**\n\nThe server responds with SYN-ACK to acknowledge the request and indicate that it is also ready.\n\n**Step 3: ACK**\n\nThe client sends an acknowledgment.\n\nThe connection is now established.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance: SYN flood\n\nA SYN flood is a denial-of-service technique that sends many TCP connection requests.\n\nThe objective is to consume resources associated with handling incomplete connections.\n\nDefenders can use:\n\n- Firewall rules.\n- SYN cookies.\n- Rate limiting.\n- Network monitoring.\n- DDoS protection services.\n\n### User Datagram Protocol (UDP)\n\nUDP is a connectionless transport protocol.\n\nUnlike TCP, it does not establish a connection through a three-way handshake.\n\nIt has less built-in transport overhead and does not provide TCP-style reliable, ordered delivery.\n\nUDP is commonly used for:\n\n- DNS queries.\n- Voice and video communication.\n- Online gaming.\n- Streaming.\n- Certain tunneling and real-time communication systems.\n\n### TCP versus UDP\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Connection setup | Connection-oriented | Connectionless |\n| Delivery guarantee | Reliability mechanisms | No built-in delivery guarantee |\n| Ordering | Maintains ordered byte stream | No built-in ordering |\n| Retransmission | Supported | Not provided by UDP itself |\n| Overhead | Generally higher | Generally lower |\n| Common uses | HTTPS over TCP, SSH, email | DNS, voice, gaming |\n\nImportant: HTTPS does not always use TCP. HTTP/3 uses QUIC, which operates over UDP.\n\n## 6. Ports and Sockets\n\nAn IP address identifies a network interface or destination at the network layer.\n\nA port identifies a particular transport-layer service or communication endpoint.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n192.168.1.10:443\n```\n\nThis represents:\n\n- IP address: `192.168.1.10`\n- Port: `443`\n\nA socket endpoint is commonly described using an IP address and a port, together with its transport protocol.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTCP 192.168.1.10:443\n```\n\n### Understanding port categories\n\n| Port range | Category |\n|---|---|\n| 0–1023 | Well-known ports |\n| 1024–49151 | Registered ports |\n| 49152–65535 | Dynamic or private ports |\n\nThese ranges are commonly used for TCP and UDP port classification.\n\n### Why ports matter in cybersecurity\n\nSuppose a server exposes these ports:\n\n```text\n22\n80\n443\n3306\n```\n\nPossible services include:\n\n| Port | Possible service |\n|---|---|\n| 22 | SSH |\n| 80 | HTTP |\n| 443 | HTTPS |\n| 3306 | MySQL |\n\nA security professional may investigate whether each service needs to be accessible.\n\nFor example, a production database should generally not be exposed directly to the public internet.\n\nThe correct approach is to restrict access through network controls and allow only authorized application servers or administrators.\n\n## 7. Internet Layer\n\nThe Internet layer is responsible for logical addressing and routing packets between networks.\n\nIts main protocol is IP.\n\nTwo major versions are:\n\n- IPv4.\n- IPv6.\n\n### IPv4\n\nIPv4 uses 32-bit addresses.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n192.168.1.10\n```\n\nIPv4 addresses are commonly written as four decimal numbers separated by dots.\n\nEach number ranges from 0 to 255.\n\n### IPv6\n\nIPv6 uses 128-bit addresses.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n2001:db8::10\n```\n\nIPv6 was developed partly to address the limitations of IPv4 address availability.\n\n### Private and public IP addresses\n\nPrivate IPv4 addresses are used within private networks and are not globally routed on the public internet.\n\nThe main private IPv4 ranges are:\n\n| Range | CIDR |\n|---|---|\n| 10.0.0.0 – 10.255.255.255 | 10.0.0.0/8 |\n| 172.16.0.0 – 172.31.255.255 | 172.16.0.0/12 |\n| 192.168.0.0 – 192.168.255.255 | 192.168.0.0/16 |\n\nPublic IP addresses are globally routable, subject to routing and network policies.\n\n### What is routing?\n\nRouting is the process of determining how packets move from one network to another.\n\nRouters examine destination IP addresses and use routing information to select an appropriate next hop.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nLaptop\n  |\n  v\nHome Router\n  |\n  v\nInternet Service Provider\n  |\n  v\nInternet Router\n  |\n  v\nWeb Server\n```\n\nThe packet may pass through several routers before reaching its destination.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nIP-related attacks and security concerns include:\n\n- IP spoofing.\n- Unauthorized network scanning.\n- Route manipulation.\n- Denial-of-service attacks.\n- Network reconnaissance.\n\nDefenders use routing controls, firewalls, anti-spoofing filters and network monitoring to reduce these risks.\n\n## 8. Network Access Layer\n\nThe Network Access layer handles communication over the local network.\n\nIt includes technologies such as:\n\n- Ethernet.\n- Wi-Fi.\n- MAC addressing.\n- Network frames.\n- Physical network transmission.\n\n### Understanding MAC addresses\n\nA MAC address is a link-layer address commonly used to identify a network interface on a local network.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E\n```\n\nA MAC address is normally associated with a network interface, although modern systems can use randomized or locally administered MAC addresses.\n\n### IP address versus MAC address\n\n| IP address | MAC address |\n|---|---|\n| Logical network address | Link-layer address |\n| Used for routing between networks | Used for local network delivery |\n| Can change when moving networks | Often associated with a network interface |\n| IPv4 uses 32 bits; IPv6 uses 128 bits | Common Ethernet MAC addresses use 48 bits |\n\n### Understanding ARP\n\nAddress Resolution Protocol (ARP) helps an IPv4 device discover the MAC address associated with a local IPv4 address.\n\nExample:\n\nA computer wants to communicate with:\n\n```text\n192.168.1.20\n```\n\nBut it does not know the destination MAC address.\n\nIt sends an ARP request on the local network.\n\nThe device using that IP address can respond with its MAC address.\n\nThe sender can then construct the appropriate Ethernet frame.\n\nARP is relevant to cybersecurity because attackers may abuse it through ARP spoofing or poisoning.\n\n### Understanding ARP spoofing\n\nARP spoofing involves sending false ARP information to associate an attacker's MAC address with another device's IP address.\n\nThis can enable interception or disruption of local network communication.\n\nDefensive measures include:\n\n- Dynamic ARP Inspection on supported switches.\n- DHCP snooping as part of a trusted switching configuration.\n- Network segmentation.\n- Monitoring unusual ARP changes.\n- Encryption of application traffic.\n\nEncryption is particularly important because local network interception does not automatically reveal protected HTTPS content.\n\n## 9. Part 1 Practical Exercise: Identify Network Layers\n\nConsider the following activity:\n\nYou open an HTTPS website from your laptop.\n\nIdentify the relevant TCP/IP layer for each operation.\n\n| Operation | TCP/IP layer |\n|---|---|\n| Browser creates an HTTP request | Application |\n| TCP manages reliable delivery | Transport |\n| IP determines packet destination | Internet |\n| Ethernet or Wi-Fi transmits a frame | Network Access |\n\n### Exercise questions\n\n1. What is the purpose of the TCP/IP model?\n2. Name the four TCP/IP layers.\n3. Explain encapsulation.\n4. What is the difference between TCP and UDP?\n5. What is the purpose of an IP address?\n6. What is the purpose of a MAC address?\n7. Explain the TCP three-way handshake.\n8. Why are ports important in cybersecurity?\n\nProgress: Part 1 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: HOW NETWORK COMMUNICATION WORKS\n\n## 10. Understanding End-to-End Network Communication\n\nNow that we understand the four layers, let's examine how they work together during real communication.\n\nImagine a developer working on a laptop wants to access a marketplace API hosted on a VPS.\n\nThe developer sends a request:\n\n```text\nhttps://api.example.com/products\n```\n\nThe request must travel from the developer's device to the remote server.\n\nThe communication involves several operations.\n\n### Stage 1: DNS Resolution\n\nThe laptop needs to determine the IP address associated with:\n\n```text\napi.example.com\n```\n\nIt sends a DNS query.\n\nA DNS resolver returns an IP address, for example:\n\n```text\n203.0.113.20\n```\n\nThis is an illustrative documentation address, not a real production destination.\n\nDNS can use UDP or TCP port 53. Encrypted DNS options also exist.\n\n### Stage 2: Determining the Local Destination\n\nThe laptop checks whether the destination is on the local network.\n\nIf the destination is outside the local subnet, the laptop normally sends the packet to its default gateway.\n\nIt needs the gateway's local link-layer address to construct the outgoing frame.\n\nFor IPv4, ARP can resolve that address.\n\nFor IPv6, Neighbor Discovery performs the corresponding address-resolution role.\n\n### Stage 3: Establishing Transport Communication\n\nIf the application uses HTTPS over HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2, it commonly establishes a TCP connection to port 443.\n\nThe TCP handshake occurs.\n\nIf the application uses HTTP/3, it may use QUIC over UDP instead.\n\n### Stage 4: Establishing Encryption\n\nFor HTTPS, TLS provides encryption and authentication.\n\nThe client and server negotiate cryptographic parameters and establish session keys.\n\nThis protects application communication against many forms of interception and tampering.\n\n### Stage 5: Sending the HTTP Request\n\nThe browser sends an HTTP request through the established secure connection.\n\nExample:\n\n```http\nGET /products HTTP/1.1\nHost: api.example.com\n```\n\nThe API receives the request and processes it.\n\n### Stage 6: Receiving the Response\n\nThe API returns a response.\n\nExample:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: application/json\n```\n\nThe browser or client application receives and processes the response.\n\n## 11. Understanding DNS Communication\n\nDNS stands for Domain Name System.\n\nIt translates domain names into records that applications and network systems can use.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nexample.com\n```\n\nMay resolve to an IPv4 address through an A record.\n\n### Important DNS record types\n\n| Record | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| A | Maps a hostname to an IPv4 address |\n| AAAA | Maps a hostname to an IPv6 address |\n| CNAME | Creates an alias to another hostname |\n| MX | Identifies mail servers |\n| NS | Identifies authoritative name servers |\n| TXT | Stores text information, often used for verification and email security |\n| PTR | Supports reverse DNS lookups |\n| SRV | Identifies services and their connection details |\n\n### Practical DNS investigation\n\nOn Linux, use:\n\n```bash\nnslookup example.com\n```\n\nThis asks a DNS resolver to resolve the domain.\n\nAnother option:\n\n```bash\ndig example.com\n```\n\n`dig` provides more detailed DNS information.\n\nTo request an IPv4 address:\n\n```bash\ndig A example.com\n```\n\nTo request an IPv6 address:\n\n```bash\ndig AAAA example.com\n```\n\nTo inspect mail records:\n\n```bash\ndig MX example.com\n```\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nDNS can be abused in different ways:\n\n- DNS spoofing.\n- DNS cache poisoning.\n- DNS tunneling.\n- Malicious domain resolution.\n- Domain generation algorithms.\n- DNS-based command-and-control communication.\n\nSecurity teams may monitor DNS requests for suspicious domains, unusual query patterns and unexpected DNS servers.\n\n## 12. Understanding DHCP\n\nDHCP stands for Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol.\n\nIt automatically provides network configuration to devices.\n\nA DHCP server may assign:\n\n- IP address.\n- Subnet mask or prefix information.\n- Default gateway.\n- DNS server addresses.\n- Lease duration.\n\n### Understanding the DHCP process\n\nThe common DHCPv4 allocation process is called DORA.\n\nIt consists of four stages:\n\n1. Discover.\n2. Offer.\n3. Request.\n4. Acknowledge.\n\nDiagram:\n\n```text\nClient                         DHCP Server\n  |                                  |\n  |---------- Discover ------------>|\n  |                                  |\n  |<----------- Offer ---------------|\n  |                                  |\n  |---------- Request -------------->|\n  |                                  |\n  |<--------- Acknowledge -----------|\n  |                                  |\n  |       Configuration Ready        |\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n**Discover**\n\nThe client broadcasts to find available DHCP servers.\n\n**Offer**\n\nA DHCP server offers an IP configuration.\n\n**Request**\n\nThe client requests the offered configuration.\n\n**Acknowledge**\n\nThe server confirms the allocation.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nAttackers may introduce unauthorized DHCP servers to distribute malicious network settings.\n\nFor example, a rogue DHCP server could provide an attacker-controlled default gateway or DNS server.\n\nDefensive measures include:\n\n- DHCP snooping.\n- Restricting access to network equipment.\n- Monitoring DHCP activity.\n- Using trusted DHCP servers.\n- Network segmentation.\n\n## 13. Understanding ICMP\n\nICMP stands for Internet Control Message Protocol.\n\nIt is used for network diagnostics and error reporting.\n\nOne common use is the `ping` command.\n\nExample:\n\n```bash\nping example.com\n```\n\nThis typically sends ICMP Echo Requests and waits for Echo Replies.\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\n64 bytes from example.com: icmp_seq=1 ttl=56 time=24 ms\n64 bytes from example.com: icmp_seq=2 ttl=56 time=23 ms\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `64 bytes`: Size of the returned payload.\n- `icmp_seq`: Sequence number of the request.\n- `ttl`: Remaining IP hop limit expressed as TTL.\n- `time`: Approximate round-trip time.\n\nA failed ping does not necessarily mean the server is offline.\n\nFirewalls may block ICMP while allowing normal application traffic.\n\n### Other ICMP uses\n\nICMP can report conditions such as:\n\n- Destination unreachable.\n- Time exceeded.\n- Packet too big.\n- Echo request and reply.\n\nIPv6 uses ICMPv6 for important functions, including Neighbor Discovery.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nICMP can be used for:\n\n- Network discovery.\n- Connectivity testing.\n- Path diagnostics.\n- Certain denial-of-service techniques.\n- Covert communication in some attack scenarios.\n\nDefenders should distinguish legitimate diagnostic traffic from suspicious patterns.\n\n## 14. Understanding Routing and the Default Gateway\n\nA default gateway is the router or network device that forwards traffic when the destination is outside the local network and no more specific route applies.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nLaptop IP:       192.168.1.10\nSubnet:          192.168.1.0/24\nDefault Gateway: 192.168.1.1\nRemote Server:   203.0.113.20\n```\n\nThe laptop determines that the remote server is outside its local subnet.\n\nIt forwards the traffic to:\n\n```text\n192.168.1.1\n```\n\nThe router then determines the next hop.\n\n### Viewing the routing table\n\nLinux:\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```text\ndefault via 192.168.1.1 dev eth0\n192.168.1.0/24 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```text\ndefault via 192.168.1.1\n```\n\nTraffic without a more specific matching route uses `192.168.1.1` as the next hop.\n\n```text\n192.168.1.0/24 dev eth0\n```\n\nIndicates that the local subnet is reachable through interface `eth0`.\n\n### Why routing matters in cybersecurity\n\nIncorrect routing can cause:\n\n- Network exposure.\n- Unauthorized communication paths.\n- Traffic interception.\n- Connectivity failures.\n- Access-control bypasses.\n\nSecurity professionals examine routing tables to understand how traffic moves through systems.\n\n## 15. Understanding NAT\n\nNAT stands for Network Address Translation.\n\nIt modifies network address information as packets pass through a network device.\n\nA common example is source NAT, especially Port Address Translation (PAT).\n\nMultiple private devices can share one public IPv4 address for outgoing internet communication.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nLaptop:      192.168.1.10\nPhone:       192.168.1.11\nTablet:      192.168.1.12\n                  |\n                  v\n              Home Router\n                  |\n                  v\n           Public IP Address\n```\n\nThe router tracks outgoing connections so that responses can be forwarded to the appropriate internal device.\n\n### Cybersecurity relevance\n\nNAT can reduce direct exposure of internal IPv4 addresses, but it is not a substitute for a firewall.\n\nSecurity still requires:\n\n- Proper firewall policies.\n- Secure device configuration.\n- Network segmentation.\n- Authentication.\n- Monitoring.\n\nNAT does not automatically make an internal system secure.\n\n## 16. Understanding TLS and HTTPS\n\nTLS stands for Transport Layer Security.\n\nIt provides cryptographic protection for communication between applications.\n\nHTTPS is HTTP carried over a secure connection using TLS.\n\nTLS provides:\n\n- Confidentiality.\n- Integrity.\n- Authentication of the server.\n- Optional client authentication in mutual TLS configurations.\n\n### Simplified HTTPS communication\n\n```text\nBrowser\n   |\n   | TLS Handshake\n   |\n   v\nSecure Session Established\n   |\n   | Encrypted HTTP Request\n   |\n   v\nWeb Server\n   |\n   | Encrypted HTTP Response\n   |\n   v\nBrowser\n```\n\n### What happens during a TLS handshake?\n\nThe simplified process includes:\n\n1. Client and server negotiate supported TLS parameters.\n2. The server presents its certificate.\n3. The client validates the certificate and hostname.\n4. Key establishment occurs.\n5. Both sides derive session keys.\n6. Protected application communication begins.\n\nModern TLS commonly uses ephemeral key exchange to establish session keys.\n\n### Why TLS matters in cybersecurity\n\nWithout encryption, network observers may be able to read or manipulate application data.\n\nTLS helps protect:\n\n- Login credentials.\n- Session cookies.\n- API requests.\n- Payment information.\n- Private messages.\n- Other application data.\n\nHowever, HTTPS does not guarantee that a website itself is trustworthy. Malicious websites can also use valid TLS certificates.\n\n## 17. Understanding Firewalls and Network Filtering\n\nA firewall controls network traffic according to configured security rules.\n\nA firewall can inspect:\n\n- Source IP addresses.\n- Destination IP addresses.\n- Source ports.\n- Destination ports.\n- Protocols.\n- Connection state.\n- In some systems, application-layer information.\n\n### Example firewall policy\n\nImagine a production API server.\n\nIts intended access policy is:\n\n| Traffic | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Public HTTPS traffic to port 443 | Allow |\n| Public SSH access | Restrict to trusted administrator IPs |\n| Public access to MongoDB port 27017 | Deny |\n| API server to MongoDB | Allow through internal network |\n| Unnecessary inbound traffic | Deny |\n\nThis follows the principle of least privilege.\n\nOnly required network communication should be permitted.\n\n### Stateful and stateless firewalls\n\n**Stateful firewall**\n\nTracks connection state and can allow response traffic belonging to an established connection.\n\n**Stateless firewall**\n\nEvaluates packets individually against rules without maintaining the same connection-state tracking.\n\nBoth approaches are useful in different network environments.\n\n## 18. Part 2 Practical Exercise: Network Communication Investigation\n\nYou will investigate how your computer communicates with other systems.\n\nRun these commands on a Linux machine or a Linux virtual machine.\n\n### Task 1: Identify IP configuration\n\n```bash\nip addr\n```\n\nIdentify:\n\n- Network interfaces.\n- IPv4 addresses.\n- IPv6 addresses.\n- Interface state.\n\n### Task 2: Inspect routing\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nIdentify the default gateway.\n\n### Task 3: Resolve a domain\n\n```bash\ndig example.com\n```\n\nIdentify the returned DNS records.\n\n### Task 4: Test connectivity\n\n```bash\nping -c 4 example.com\n```\n\nSend four ICMP Echo Requests.\n\n### Task 5: Check an HTTPS connection\n\n```bash\ncurl -I https://example.com\n```\n\nThe `-I` option requests response headers.\n\n### Task 6: Trace a network path\n\n```bash\ntraceroute example.com\n```\n\nIf the command is not installed, install an appropriate package for your Linux distribution or use an available alternative such as:\n\n```bash\ntracepath example.com\n```\n\n### Task 7: Inspect listening ports\n\n```bash\nss -tuln\n```\n\nIdentify listening TCP and UDP sockets.\n\n### Task 8: Investigate a TCP connection\n\n```bash\nnc -vz example.com 443\n```\n\nThis attempts to connect to TCP port 443.\n\nA successful connection indicates that the TCP connection attempt succeeded. It does not prove that the application or TLS configuration is fully secure.\n\nProgress: Part 2 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: NETWORK ANALYSIS AND CYBERSECURITY PRACTICALS\n\n## 19. Why Cybersecurity Professionals Study Network Traffic\n\nNetwork traffic contains valuable information about communication between devices.\n\nBy analysing traffic, security professionals can investigate:\n\n- Suspicious connections.\n- Unauthorized services.\n- Malware communication.\n- Data exfiltration.\n- Network scanning.\n- DNS abuse.\n- Unusual authentication activity.\n- Denial-of-service attempts.\n\nNetwork analysis is an important part of:\n\n- Security operations centres (SOCs).\n- Incident response.\n- Penetration testing.\n- Digital forensics.\n- Network defence.\n- Threat hunting.\n\n## 20. Understanding Packet Analysis\n\nA packet is a formatted unit of data transmitted using a network protocol.\n\nPacket analysis involves examining captured network traffic to understand what devices are communicating and how.\n\nA network packet may contain:\n\n| Field | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Source IP | Identifies the packet's source address |\n| Destination IP | Identifies the intended destination |\n| Protocol | Identifies the network or transport protocol |\n| Source port | Identifies the sending application endpoint |\n| Destination port | Identifies the receiving application endpoint |\n| Flags | Provides control information, particularly in TCP |\n| Payload | Contains protocol data |\n\nNot every packet has the same fields. The structure depends on the protocols being used.\n\n### Example packet information\n\nImagine a TCP packet with:\n\n```text\nSource IP:      192.168.1.10\nDestination IP: 203.0.113.20\nSource Port:    51500\nDestination Port: 443\nProtocol:       TCP\nFlags:          SYN\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- A local device is attempting to contact a remote destination.\n- The source port is `51500`.\n- The destination port is `443`.\n- The SYN flag indicates a TCP connection initiation request.\n\nThis could be a normal HTTPS connection attempt.\n\nThe same information, when combined with unusual frequency or destinations, might also contribute to an investigation.\n\n## 21. Understanding Wireshark\n\nWireshark is a network protocol analyser.\n\nIt captures and displays network traffic so that analysts can inspect individual packets and protocol conversations.\n\nOfficial website: https://www.wireshark.org/\n\nIt is widely used for:\n\n- Network troubleshooting.\n- Protocol analysis.\n- Incident investigation.\n- Security research.\n- Learning networking concepts.\n\n### Basic Wireshark workflow\n\n1. Open Wireshark.\n2. Select an authorized network interface.\n3. Start packet capture.\n4. Generate network activity.\n5. Stop the capture.\n6. Apply display filters.\n7. Inspect packet details.\n\nOnly capture traffic on networks and devices you own or are explicitly authorized to monitor. A packet capture can contain private information.\n\n### Important Wireshark display filters\n\n| Filter | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `ip` | Displays IPv4 packets |\n| `ipv6` | Displays IPv6 packets |\n| `tcp` | Displays TCP packets |\n| `udp` | Displays UDP packets |\n| `dns` | Displays DNS traffic |\n| `http` | Displays HTTP traffic |\n| `tls` | Displays TLS traffic |\n| `icmp` | Displays ICMP traffic |\n| `tcp.port == 443` | Displays TCP packets using port 443 |\n| `ip.addr == 192.168.1.10` | Displays packets involving an IP address |\n| `dns.qry.name == \"example.com\"` | Displays matching DNS queries |\n\nThese are display filters, not capture filters.\n\n### Example investigation\n\nSuppose you want to observe DNS traffic generated by your own computer.\n\nApply:\n\n```text\ndns\n```\n\nThen visit an allowed website.\n\nWireshark may display DNS requests and responses.\n\nYou can inspect:\n\n- Query name.\n- Query type.\n- DNS response.\n- Returned IP address.\n- Response time.\n\nIf the traffic uses encrypted DNS, ordinary DNS queries may not be visible in the same way.\n\n## 22. Using tcpdump for Command-Line Packet Analysis\n\n`tcpdump` is a command-line packet analyser.\n\nIt is useful on Linux servers where graphical tools may not be available.\n\n### Display network interfaces\n\n```bash\nip link\n```\n\nIdentify the interface you want to monitor.\n\n### Capture ICMP traffic\n\n```bash\nsudo tcpdump -i eth0 icmp\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `sudo` requests elevated privileges.\n- `tcpdump` starts packet capture.\n- `-i eth0` selects the network interface.\n- `icmp` filters the captured packets to ICMP traffic.\n\nReplace `eth0` with the actual interface name on your system.\n\n### Capture DNS traffic\n\n```bash\nsudo tcpdump -i eth0 port 53\n```\n\nThis captures traffic involving port 53.\n\n### Capture TCP traffic on port 443\n\n```bash\nsudo tcpdump -i eth0 tcp port 443\n```\n\nThis captures matching TCP traffic.\n\nIt will not capture QUIC-based HTTP/3 traffic, which commonly uses UDP port 443.\n\n### Save a capture to a file\n\n```bash\nsudo tcpdump -i eth0 -w network-capture.pcap\n```\n\nThe `-w` option writes packets to a capture file.\n\nStop the capture with `Ctrl+C`.\n\nRead the saved capture:\n\n```bash\ntcpdump -r network-capture.pcap\n```\n\nThis reads packets from the saved file instead of capturing live traffic.\n\n## 23. Understanding Nmap and Network Discovery\n\nNmap is a network exploration and security auditing tool.\n\nIt can identify:\n\n- Hosts that respond to discovery probes.\n- Open ports.\n- Network services.\n- Service versions.\n- Certain operating system characteristics.\n\nOfficial documentation: https://nmap.org/book/man.html\n\nUse it only on systems and networks you own or are authorized to assess.\n\n### Basic scan of your own machine\n\n```bash\nnmap 127.0.0.1\n```\n\nThis scans the local host.\n\n### Scan a specific authorized host\n\n```bash\nnmap 192.168.1.10\n```\n\nThis checks common ports on the specified host.\n\n### Scan selected ports\n\n```bash\nnmap -p 22,80,443 192.168.1.10\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `nmap` runs the scanner.\n- `-p` specifies the ports.\n- `22,80,443` selects SSH, HTTP and HTTPS ports.\n- `192.168.1.10` is the target.\n\n### Detect service versions\n\n```bash\nnmap -sV 192.168.1.10\n```\n\nThe `-sV` option attempts to identify services and their versions.\n\n### Understanding scan results\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nPORT    STATE  SERVICE\n22/tcp  open   ssh\n80/tcp  open   http\n443/tcp open   https\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- TCP port 22 is accepting connections.\n- TCP port 80 is accepting connections.\n- TCP port 443 is accepting connections.\n\nAn open port does not automatically mean a vulnerability exists.\n\nThe next step is to understand whether the service is required, correctly configured and securely maintained.\n\n## 24. Understanding Network Scanning Attacks\n\nNetwork scanning is also used during malicious reconnaissance.\n\nAttackers may scan systems to identify:\n\n- Exposed ports.\n- Running services.\n- Operating system information.\n- Network devices.\n- Potential entry points.\n\nDefenders can identify suspicious scanning through:\n\n- Firewall logs.\n- Intrusion detection systems.\n- Network flow records.\n- Repeated connection attempts.\n- Unusual connection patterns.\n\n### Example: Recognizing a port scan\n\nSuppose firewall logs show:\n\n```text\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:21\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:22\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:23\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:25\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:80\n192.168.1.50 -> 192.168.1.100:443\n```\n\nThe same source is attempting to reach several ports on one destination.\n\nThis may indicate service discovery or port scanning.\n\nHowever, legitimate administrators and monitoring systems can generate similar patterns.\n\nSecurity analysts should investigate context instead of assuming every scan is malicious.\n\n## 25. Understanding Common Network Attacks\n\n### A. Man-in-the-Middle (MitM)\n\nA man-in-the-middle attack occurs when an attacker positions themselves between communicating parties to intercept or manipulate communication.\n\nPossible enabling conditions include:\n\n- Compromised network equipment.\n- Rogue wireless access points.\n- ARP spoofing.\n- DNS manipulation.\n- Insecure communication channels.\n\nDefensive measures:\n\n- Use HTTPS and correctly configured TLS.\n- Validate certificates.\n- Secure wireless networks.\n- Use VPNs where appropriate.\n- Apply network segmentation.\n- Monitor suspicious network changes.\n\n### B. ARP Spoofing\n\nAn attacker sends false ARP information to influence how devices associate IP addresses with MAC addresses.\n\nPossible consequences include:\n\n- Traffic interception.\n- Traffic redirection.\n- Network disruption.\n\nDefensive measures include Dynamic ARP Inspection, trusted network infrastructure and monitoring.\n\n### C. DNS Spoofing\n\nAn attacker attempts to provide false DNS information so that a victim connects to an unintended destination.\n\nDefensive measures include:\n\n- Secure DNS infrastructure.\n- DNSSEC validation where applicable.\n- Encrypted DNS where appropriate.\n- Monitoring suspicious DNS responses.\n- Strong endpoint and network security.\n\nDNSSEC provides authenticity and integrity protections for DNS data; it does not encrypt DNS queries.\n\n### D. SYN Flood\n\nAn attacker sends large volumes of TCP SYN requests to consume server resources.\n\nPossible defensive measures:\n\n- SYN cookies.\n- Rate limiting.\n- Firewall controls.\n- DDoS mitigation.\n- Capacity planning.\n\n### E. Packet Sniffing\n\nPacket sniffing involves capturing and inspecting network traffic.\n\nIt can be legitimate for troubleshooting and security monitoring, but unauthorized capture can expose private information.\n\nDefensive measures:\n\n- Encrypt sensitive communication.\n- Secure network access.\n- Use switched network infrastructure appropriately.\n- Restrict packet capture privileges.\n- Monitor unauthorized monitoring devices.\n\n## 26. Understanding Network Segmentation\n\nNetwork segmentation divides a network into smaller sections with controlled communication between them.\n\nConsider a marketplace infrastructure:\n\n```text\n             Internet\n                 |\n                 v\n             Firewall\n                 |\n                 v\n           Public Subnet\n                 |\n                 v\n           Reverse Proxy\n                 |\n                 v\n         Private Application\n                 |\n                 v\n          Private Database\n```\n\nThe database is not directly accessible from the public internet.\n\nOnly approved application systems can communicate with it.\n\n### Benefits of segmentation\n\n- Limits unauthorized access.\n- Reduces lateral movement.\n- Isolates sensitive systems.\n- Simplifies monitoring.\n- Supports access-control policies.\n\n### Example security policy\n\n| Source | Destination | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Internet | Reverse proxy, TCP 443 | Allow |\n| Internet | MongoDB, TCP 27017 | Deny |\n| API server | MongoDB, TCP 27017 | Allow |\n| Administrator network | SSH, TCP 22 | Allow |\n| Unapproved network | Internal database | Deny |\n\nThe rules should be implemented in the appropriate firewall, cloud security group or network access-control system.\n\n## 27. Practical Lab: Analyse Your Own Network\n\nThis lab uses tools commonly available on Linux.\n\n### Lab objective\n\nInvestigate how your own computer communicates with the network.\n\n### Step 1: Identify your interfaces\n\n```bash\nip addr\n```\n\nRecord:\n\n- Interface names.\n- IP addresses.\n- Interface status.\n\n### Step 2: Identify the default gateway\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nRecord the default gateway.\n\n### Step 3: Identify DNS information\n\nDepending on your Linux distribution and configuration:\n\n```bash\nresolvectl status\n```\n\nThis displays resolver information when systemd-resolved is being used.\n\nYou can also inspect:\n\n```bash\ncat /etc/resolv.conf\n```\n\nNote that this file may point to a local stub resolver rather than directly displaying the upstream DNS server.\n\n### Step 4: Test domain resolution\n\n```bash\ndig example.com\n```\n\nRecord the response.\n\n### Step 5: Test HTTPS connectivity\n\n```bash\ncurl -I https://example.com\n```\n\nObserve the HTTP response headers.\n\n### Step 6: Capture your own DNS traffic\n\nOpen one terminal:\n\n```bash\nsudo tcpdump -i any -n port 53\n```\n\nOpen another terminal and run:\n\n```bash\ndig example.com\n```\n\nObserve whether DNS traffic appears.\n\nSome systems use encrypted DNS or local proxy resolvers, so the capture may not show a conventional upstream DNS request.\n\nStop the capture using `Ctrl+C`.\n\n### Step 7: Examine a packet capture\n\nOpen the capture in Wireshark if you have saved one.\n\nInvestigate:\n\n- Source address.\n- Destination address.\n- Protocol.\n- Ports.\n- Packet timing.\n\n### Step 8: Perform a local port scan\n\nStart a simple test server in one terminal:\n\n```bash\npython3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1\n```\n\nThis starts a local HTTP server bound only to the loopback interface.\n\nIn another terminal:\n\n```bash\nnmap -p 8000 127.0.0.1\n```\n\nExpected result:\n\n```text\n8000/tcp open\n```\n\nThe test demonstrates how a listening service can be identified.\n\nStop the Python server using `Ctrl+C`.\n\n## 28. Part 3 Practical Challenge: Network Security Investigation\n\n### Scenario\n\nYou are a cybersecurity analyst investigating a marketplace server.\n\nThe following observations have been reported:\n\n1. Several devices are generating repeated DNS queries to unusual domains.\n2. One internal machine is attempting connections to many server ports.\n3. A database port is accessible from an unauthorized network.\n4. The server is receiving an unusually high number of TCP SYN packets.\n5. An administrator reports unexpected changes in the ARP table.\n\n### Your tasks\n\nFor each observation:\n\n- Identify the possible network issue.\n- Identify the TCP/IP layer or layers involved.\n- Explain how you would investigate it.\n- Name a suitable tool or monitoring source.\n- Recommend at least one defensive measure.\n\nDo not assume that every observation proves an attack. Your task is to collect evidence and establish what is happening.\n\nProgress: Part 3 of 4\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT — TCP/IP MODEL AND NETWORK COMMUNICATION\n\n## 29. Assessment Instructions\n\nThis assessment evaluates your understanding of network communication and its application in cybersecurity.\n\nAnswer all questions.\n\nFor technical questions, explain your reasoning rather than memorizing definitions.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 1**\n\nWhat does TCP/IP stand for?\n\nA. Transfer Control Internet Process.\n\nB. Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol.\n\nC. Transport Communication Internal Protocol.\n\nD. Transmission Computer Internet Program.\n\n**Question 2**\n\nHow many layers are in the commonly taught TCP/IP model?\n\nA. Two.\n\nB. Three.\n\nC. Four.\n\nD. Seven.\n\n**Question 3**\n\nWhich layer is responsible for logical addressing and routing?\n\nA. Application.\n\nB. Transport.\n\nC. Internet.\n\nD. Network Access.\n\n**Question 4**\n\nWhich protocol provides reliable, ordered transport communication?\n\nA. UDP.\n\nB. TCP.\n\nC. ARP.\n\nD. ICMP.\n\n**Question 5**\n\nWhich protocol is commonly used to resolve domain names?\n\nA. DNS.\n\nB. SSH.\n\nC. FTP.\n\nD. ARP.\n\n**Question 6**\n\nWhich protocol helps an IPv4 device discover the MAC address associated with a local IP address?\n\nA. DHCP.\n\nB. TCP.\n\nC. ARP.\n\nD. HTTP.\n\n**Question 7**\n\nWhich TCP flag is used to initiate a connection?\n\nA. FIN.\n\nB. RST.\n\nC. ACK.\n\nD. SYN.\n\n**Question 8**\n\nWhich command displays the Linux routing table?\n\nA. `ip route`\n\nB. `ip scan`\n\nC. `docker ps`\n\nD. `ls route`\n\n**Question 9**\n\nWhich tool is commonly used for packet analysis?\n\nA. Wireshark.\n\nB. Word.\n\nC. Excel.\n\nD. Git.\n\n**Question 10**\n\nWhich protocol is commonly used to provide secure web communication?\n\nA. HTTP without TLS.\n\nB. HTTPS.\n\nC. FTP.\n\nD. Telnet.\n\n## SECTION B: SHORT-ANSWER QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 11**\n\nExplain the purpose of the TCP/IP model.\n\n**Question 12**\n\nName the four TCP/IP layers and explain the responsibility of each.\n\n**Question 13**\n\nExplain encapsulation and decapsulation.\n\n**Question 14**\n\nDifferentiate between TCP and UDP.\n\n**Question 15**\n\nExplain the TCP three-way handshake.\n\n**Question 16**\n\nDifferentiate between an IP address and a MAC address.\n\n**Question 17**\n\nExplain the purpose of port numbers.\n\n**Question 18**\n\nWhat is the role of a default gateway?\n\n**Question 19**\n\nExplain the purpose of DNS and identify four important DNS record types.\n\n**Question 20**\n\nExplain the DHCP DORA process.\n\n## SECTION C: CYBERSECURITY QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 21**\n\nExplain how ARP spoofing can affect communication between devices on a local network.\n\n**Question 22**\n\nWhat is a SYN flood, and how can network defenders reduce its impact?\n\n**Question 23**\n\nExplain the purpose of TLS in HTTPS communication.\n\n**Question 24**\n\nWhat is a man-in-the-middle attack? Identify three defensive measures.\n\n**Question 25**\n\nExplain why NAT should not be treated as a replacement for a firewall.\n\n**Question 26**\n\nWhat is network segmentation, and how does it improve security?\n\n**Question 27**\n\nExplain how a cybersecurity analyst can identify suspicious network scanning activity.\n\n**Question 28**\n\nWhy should security professionals investigate unusual DNS traffic?\n\n## SECTION D: PRACTICAL COMMAND QUESTIONS\n\n**Question 29**\n\nWrite a Linux command that displays network interfaces and their IP addresses.\n\n**Question 30**\n\nWrite a command that displays the system's routing table.\n\n**Question 31**\n\nWrite a command that resolves `example.com` using DNS.\n\n**Question 32**\n\nWrite a command that sends four ICMP ping requests to `example.com`.\n\n**Question 33**\n\nWrite a command that checks whether TCP port 443 is accepting connections on `example.com`.\n\n**Question 34**\n\nWrite a command that displays listening TCP and UDP sockets.\n\n**Question 35**\n\nWrite a `tcpdump` command that captures DNS traffic on all available interfaces.\n\n**Question 36**\n\nWrite an Nmap command that scans TCP ports 22, 80 and 443 on an authorized test machine at `192.168.1.10`.\n\n## SECTION E: NETWORK INVESTIGATION SCENARIOS\n\n**Question 37**\n\nA user reports that they can access a website using its IP address but cannot access it using its domain name.\n\nIdentify the likely problem and explain how you would investigate it.\n\n**Question 38**\n\nA server has an open TCP port 3306, and the database is accessible from the public internet.\n\nExplain the security risk and recommend a safer network configuration.\n\n**Question 39**\n\nA network analyst observes many TCP SYN packets directed at a web server.\n\nWhat might this indicate? How would you distinguish normal connection activity from a possible SYN flood?\n\n**Question 40**\n\nA workstation unexpectedly begins sending DNS queries to an unfamiliar external resolver.\n\nExplain how you would investigate the activity.\n\n**Question 41**\n\nA user reports that their network connection is slow.\n\nDescribe how you would use `ping`, `traceroute`, `ip route`, `dig` and `curl` to investigate the problem.\n\n**Question 42**\n\nYou suspect ARP spoofing on a local network.\n\nWhat evidence would you collect, and which defensive measures would you recommend?\n\n## SECTION F: FINAL PRACTICAL PROJECT\n\n### Project: Network Communication and Cybersecurity Analysis Lab\n\nBuild a small authorized network investigation environment.\n\nYour project should demonstrate the following skills.\n\n| Area | Practical requirement |\n|---|---|\n| Network configuration | Identify interfaces, IP addresses and gateways |\n| DNS | Resolve domains and examine DNS records |\n| Routing | Inspect routing tables and network paths |\n| Connectivity | Test ICMP and application connectivity |\n| Transport | Identify TCP and UDP communication |\n| Ports | Inspect listening services |\n| Packet analysis | Capture and inspect network traffic |\n| Service discovery | Scan an authorized local test machine |\n| Security analysis | Identify possible suspicious communication |\n| Defence | Recommend appropriate network controls |\n\n### Project tasks\n\n1. Identify your Linux network interfaces.\n2. Record your IP configuration.\n3. Identify your default gateway.\n4. Resolve a domain using `dig`.\n5. Test connectivity using `ping`.\n6. Inspect an HTTPS response using `curl`.\n7. Inspect listening services using `ss`.\n8. Capture DNS traffic using `tcpdump`.\n9. Examine a packet capture using Wireshark.\n10. Start a local test service and identify its listening port.\n11. Scan your own machine or another explicitly authorized test system using Nmap.\n12. Write a short investigation report identifying possible security weaknesses and recommended protections.\n\n### Expected project report\n\nYour report should contain:\n\n- Lab objective.\n- Network topology or a simple network diagram.\n- Tools used.\n- Commands executed.\n- Observations.\n- Packet analysis findings.\n- Identified risks.\n- Recommended security controls.\n- Conclusion.\n\n## 30. Lesson Completion Checklist\n\nBefore completing this lesson, ensure that you can:\n\n- [ ] Explain the TCP/IP model.\n- [ ] Identify all four TCP/IP layers.\n- [ ] Explain encapsulation and decapsulation.\n- [ ] Differentiate TCP and UDP.\n- [ ] Explain the TCP three-way handshake.\n- [ ] Understand IP addresses and MAC addresses.\n- [ ] Explain port numbers and sockets.\n- [ ] Understand DNS resolution.\n- [ ] Explain DHCP and DORA.\n- [ ] Understand ICMP and network diagnostics.\n- [ ] Explain routing and default gateways.\n- [ ] Understand NAT.\n- [ ] Explain TLS and HTTPS.\n- [ ] Understand firewall filtering.\n- [ ] Use Wireshark for packet analysis.\n- [ ] Use tcpdump for command-line captures.\n- [ ] Use Nmap for authorized service discovery.\n- [ ] Recognize common network attacks.\n- [ ] Explain network segmentation.\n- [ ] Investigate basic network communication problems.\n\n## 31. Final Submission to Kai\n\nSubmit your answers to Questions 1–42 together with your practical project report.\n\nKai should evaluate:\n\n1. Understanding of networking fundamentals.\n2. Knowledge of TCP/IP layers.\n3. Understanding of network protocols.\n4. Ability to explain network communication.\n5. Correct use of Linux networking commands.\n6. Packet analysis skills.\n7. Cybersecurity awareness.\n8. Ability to investigate network problems.\n9. Quality of defensive recommendations.\n\nKai should mark incorrect answers, explain the correct concepts and recommend additional exercises where necessary.\n\n**Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete**\n\n**Next recommended lesson:**\n\n### Networking for Cybersecurity: IP Addressing, Subnetting and CIDR";
